text:
Receiving a comment
Apps Script calls doPost for every POST to the /exec URL. Without a token and repository it refuses at once.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 15–20
function doPost(e) {
  try {
    const props = PropertiesService.getScriptProperties();
    const token = props.getProperty("GITHUB_TOKEN");
    const repo = props.getProperty("GITHUB_REPO");
    if (!token || !repo) return reply_({ ok: false, error: "Commenting is not configured yet." });

The page sends JSON labelled as plain text (see Posting), so it is parsed by hand.

apps-script/Code.gs, line 22
const d = JSON.parse((e && e.postData && e.postData.contents) || "{}");

The checks then run cheapest first. A filled-in honeypot gets a fake success, so a bot has no reason to try again.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 24–25
// Honeypot filled in: pretend success, do nothing.
if (d.website) return reply_({ ok: true });


apps-script/Code.gs, lines 27–30
const passcode = props.getProperty("PASSCODE");
if (passcode && d.passcode !== passcode) {
  return reply_({ ok: false, error: "That passcode is not correct." });
}

SITE_PREFIX compares the page address the browser reported. It turns away other sites and casual reuse of the URL, but the address is supplied by the sender, so it is not proof of where a request came from (see Security and privacy).

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 32–36
const prefix = props.getProperty("SITE_PREFIX");
const page = clean_(d.page, 500);
if (prefix && page.indexOf(prefix) !== 0) {
  return reply_({ ok: false, error: "Comments are not accepted from this page." });
}

The name and comment are trimmed and cut to the same limits the form uses, since a request need not come from the form.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 38–40
const name = clean_(d.name, 80).replace(/\s+/g, " ");
const comment = clean_(d.comment, 5000);
if (!name || !comment) return reply_({ ok: false, error: "Please enter your name and a comment." });

The rate limit is checked last, so that rejected requests don’t use it up.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 42–44
if (!allow_(Number(props.getProperty("MAX_PER_HOUR")) || 20)) {
  return reply_({ ok: false, error: "Too many comments right now. Please try again later." });
}

Then the issue is created, and the reader is sent its number and address.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 46–48
const issue = createIssue_(token, repo, buildIssue_(d, name, comment, page),
  props.getProperty("LABEL") || "reader-comment");
return reply_({ ok: true, number: issue.number, url: issue.html_url });

Any error, including one from GitHub, is written to the Apps Script execution log for you, and the reader gets a general message, so GitHub’s response is never shown on the page.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 49–52
} catch (err) {
  console.error(err);
  return reply_({ ok: false, error: "Something went wrong. Please try again." });
}

Checking the deployment

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 55–58
/** Lets you open the /exec URL in a browser to check the deployment works. */
function doGet() {
  return reply_({ ok: true, message: "issue-comments relay is running." });
}

Building the issue
buildIssue_ writes the title and body. The anchor is reduced to characters valid in an id, so it cannot break out of the link. The name and section are escaped so they display literally; the passage and comment are left as typed, so GitHub renders any Markdown in them. Finally noMentions_ runs over both title and body.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 60–81
/** The page previews this text before posting: keep issueTitle() and
 *  fillPreview() in issue-comments.js in step with any change here. */
function buildIssue_(d, name, comment, page) {
  const section = clean_(d.section, 200).replace(/\s+/g, " ");
  const anchor = clean_(d.anchor, 200).replace(/[^A-Za-z0-9_\-:.]/g, "");
  const quote = clean_(d.quote, 1600);
  const link = page + (anchor ? "#" + anchor : "");

  const firstWords = comment.replace(/\s+/g, " ").slice(0, 60) + (comment.length &gt; 60 ? "…" : "");
  const title = (section ? "[" + section.slice(0, 60) + "] " : "") + firstWords;

  const lines = [
    "**Submitted by:** " + escapeInline_(name) + ", via the site's comment form",
    "**Section:** [" + escapeInline_(section || "Page") + "](" + link + ")",
    "",
  ];
  if (quote) {
    lines.push(quote.split("\n").map(function (l) { return "&gt; " + l; }).join("\n"), "");
  }
  lines.push(comment);
  return { title: noMentions_(title), body: noMentions_(lines.join("\n")) };
}







Where the preview and the issue can differ



The preview’s title comes from issueTitle in the browser script, and there are two small differences from the title built here:

The … is added here when the comment is more than 60 characters before runs of spaces and line breaks are collapsed, and in the browser when it is more than 60 characters after. A comment a little under 60 characters that contains several line breaks can get a … in the issue but not in the preview.
Here the section heading has runs of spaces collapsed before it is cut to 60 characters; in the browser it is used as it is.

Neither changes what a reader wrote, only how the title is trimmed. They are noted here so that the two copies can be brought back into step.

Creating the issue
createIssue_ calls the GitHub REST API with the token and the reader-comment label (or your LABEL). muteHttpExceptions lets the code read GitHub’s error instead of Apps Script throwing its own, and anything other than 201 Created is treated as a failure.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 83–99
function createIssue_(token, repo, issue, label) {
  const res = UrlFetchApp.fetch("https://api.github.com/repos/" + repo + "/issues", {
    method: "post",
    contentType: "application/json",
    headers: {
      Authorization: "Bearer " + token,
      Accept: "application/vnd.github+json",
      "X-GitHub-Api-Version": "2022-11-28",
    },
    payload: JSON.stringify({ title: issue.title, body: issue.body, labels: [label] }),
    muteHttpExceptions: true,
  });
  if (res.getResponseCode() !== 201) {
    throw new Error("GitHub " + res.getResponseCode() + ": " + res.getContentText().slice(0, 300));
  }
  return JSON.parse(res.getContentText());
}

The hourly limit
allow_ keeps one counter per clock hour in the script cache. The script lock stops two simultaneous requests from both reading the same count and both going through. The cache entry lasts a little over an hour (3,700 seconds), long enough to cover its hour.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 101–115
/** Simple shared limit: at most `max` issues per rolling hour. */
function allow_(max) {
  const lock = LockService.getScriptLock();
  lock.waitLock(5000);
  try {
    const cache = CacheService.getScriptCache();
    const key = "count-" + Math.floor(Date.now() / 3600000);
    const n = Number(cache.get(key) || 0);
    if (n &gt;= max) return false;
    cache.put(key, String(n + 1), 3700);
    return true;
  } finally {
    lock.releaseLock();
  }
}

The comment above it says “rolling hour”, but the counter resets at the top of each clock hour, so up to twice MAX_PER_HOUR can arrive within one 60-minute span that straddles the change. Google may also clear the cache early, which resets the count.

Small helpers
clean_ turns anything into a trimmed string with Unix line endings, cut to a maximum length.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 117–119
function clean_(v, max) {
  return String(v == null ? "" : v).replace(/\r\n?/g, "\n").trim().slice(0, max);
}

noMentions_ puts an invisible zero-width space after each @ that is followed by a letter or digit. The text looks the same, but GitHub no longer sees @username, so a comment cannot notify anyone from your account.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 122–124
function noMentions_(s) {
  return s.replace(/@(?=[A-Za-z0-9])/g, "@​");
}

escapeInline_ puts a backslash before Markdown’s special characters, so a name such as *Jane* shows as typed.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 126–128
function escapeInline_(s) {
  return s.replace(/([\\`*_\[\]&lt;&gt;])/g, "\\$1");
}

reply_ sends every answer as JSON.

apps-script/Code.gs, lines 130–133
function reply_(obj) {
  return ContentService.createTextOutput(JSON.stringify(obj))
    .setMimeType(ContentService.MimeType.JSON);
}

Buttons
Both buttons are rounded “pills” with a light shadow. The selection button is placed absolutely, at the position the script calculates; the page button is fixed to the bottom right of the window. A z-index of 1050 puts them above Bootstrap’s fixed navigation bar, which uses 1030.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.css, lines 1–18
.ic-select-btn,
.ic-page-btn {
  font: inherit;
  font-size: 0.85rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color, #ccc);
  background: var(--bs-body-bg, #fff);
  color: var(--bs-body-color, #222);
  border-radius: 999px;
  padding: 0.3rem 0.8rem;
  box-shadow: 0 2px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.15);
  cursor: pointer;
  z-index: 1050;
}
.ic-select-btn { position: absolute; }
.ic-page-btn { position: fixed; right: 1rem; bottom: 1rem; }
.ic-select-btn:hover,
.ic-page-btn:hover { border-color: var(--bs-primary, #2780e3); }
.ic-select-btn[hidden] { display: none; }

The [hidden] rules matter because a display value set by a stylesheet overrides the browser’s built-in handling of the hidden attribute, which the script uses to show and hide things.

The dialog
The dialog is at most 34rem wide, and narrower on small screens. The backdrop dims the page behind it.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.css, lines 20–28
.ic-dialog {
  width: min(34rem, calc(100vw - 2rem));
  border: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color, #ccc);
  border-radius: 0.5rem;
  padding: 1.25rem;
  background: var(--bs-body-bg, #fff);
  color: var(--bs-body-color, #222);
}
.ic-dialog::backdrop { background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35); }

The heading’s border and padding are reset because Quarto themes add a rule under h2. Labels sit above full-width fields.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.css, lines 29–43
.ic-dialog h2 { font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; border: 0; padding: 0; }
.ic-dialog label { display: block; margin: 0.6rem 0; font-weight: 500; }
.ic-dialog input,
.ic-dialog textarea {
  display: block;
  width: 100%;
  margin-top: 0.25rem;
  font: inherit;
  font-weight: normal;
  padding: 0.4rem 0.5rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color, #ccc);
  border-radius: 0.3rem;
  background: var(--bs-body-bg, #fff);
  color: inherit;
}

Passage, notes and the honeypot
The quoted passage keeps its line breaks and scrolls once it is taller than 9rem. The honeypot field is moved far off-screen rather than hidden with display: none, because bots may skip fields that are not displayed.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.css, lines 44–61
.ic-quote {
  max-height: 9rem;
  overflow: auto;
  margin: 0.5rem 0;
  padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem;
  border-left: 3px solid var(--bs-primary, #2780e3);
  background: var(--bs-tertiary-bg, #f6f6f6);
  font-size: 0.9rem;
  white-space: pre-wrap;
}
.ic-quote[hidden] { display: none; }
.ic-section { margin: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.75; }
.ic-note { font-size: 0.8rem; opacity: 0.75; margin: 0.5rem 0; }
.ic-trap { position: absolute !important; left: -10000px; width: 1px; height: 1px; opacity: 0; }
.ic-status { min-height: 1.2em; font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0.5rem 0; }
.ic-ok { color: var(--bs-success, #2e7d32); }
.ic-error { color: var(--bs-danger, #c62828); }
.ic-note a { color: var(--bs-link-color, #2780e3); }

Preview card and actions

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.css, lines 62–89
.ic-pv-card {
  border: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color, #ccc);
  border-radius: 0.4rem;
  padding: 0.75rem 0.9rem;
  margin: 0.5rem 0;
  max-height: 50vh;
  overflow: auto;
}
.ic-pv-title { font-weight: 600; margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
.ic-pv-body p { margin: 0.3rem 0; font-size: 0.9rem; }
.ic-pv-comment { white-space: pre-wrap; }
.ic-actions { display: flex; justify-content: flex-end; gap: 0.5rem; }
.ic-actions button {
  font: inherit;
  padding: 0.35rem 0.9rem;
  border-radius: 0.3rem;
  border: 1px solid var(--bs-border-color, #ccc);
  background: transparent;
  color: inherit;
  cursor: pointer;
}
.ic-actions .ic-primary {
  background: var(--bs-primary, #2780e3);
  border-color: var(--bs-primary, #2780e3);
  color: #fff;
}
.ic-actions button[hidden] { display: none; }
.ic-actions button:disabled { opacity: 0.6; cursor: default; }

Printing
The buttons and dialog are hidden when a page is printed.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.css, lines 91–93
@media print {
  .ic-select-btn, .ic-page-btn, .ic-dialog { display: none !important; }
}

Helpers
store reads or saves one value in localStorage. It is wrapped in try because some browsers throw when storage is blocked (private windows, strict privacy settings); the script then simply doesn’t remember the name.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 9–16
function store(key, value) {
  try {
    if (value === undefined) return window.localStorage.getItem(key) || "";
    window.localStorage.setItem(key, value);
  } catch (e) {
    return "";
  }
}

el builds every element the script adds. Text always goes in through textContent or text nodes, never innerHTML, so nothing a reader types and no setting can become markup.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 18–28
function el(tag, attrs, children) {
  const node = document.createElement(tag);
  Object.entries(attrs || {}).forEach(([k, v]) =&gt; {
    if (k === "text") node.textContent = v;
    else if (v !== false && v != null) node.setAttribute(k, v === true ? "" : v);
  });
  (children || []).forEach((c) =&gt;
    node.appendChild(typeof c === "string" ? document.createTextNode(c) : c)
  );
  return node;
}

safeLink accepts the contact setting only as an ordinary web, mail or relative link, so a javascript: address in a site’s settings cannot run when a reader clicks it.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 30–33
// Only allow ordinary links for the contact address.
function safeLink(url) {
  return /^(https?:|mailto:|\/|\.\/|\.\.\/)/i.test(url || "") ? url : null;
}

issueTitle builds the issue title for the preview: the section in brackets, then the first 60 characters of the comment. The relay builds the real title with its own copy of this logic.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 35–40
// Must match buildIssue_ in apps-script/Code.gs.
function issueTitle(section, comment) {
  const flat = comment.replace(/\s+/g, " ");
  const words = flat.slice(0, 60) + (flat.length &gt; 60 ? "…" : "");
  return (section ? "[" + section.slice(0, 60) + "] " : "") + words;
}

Starting up
init runs once the page has loaded. Without an endpoint it does nothing, so a page where the filter ran but the settings are incomplete is unaffected. Selections are only accepted inside root, which is Quarto’s main content area when present; this keeps the sidebar, table of contents and footer out.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 42–44
function init() {
  const cfg = window.ISSUE_COMMENTS || {};
  if (!cfg.endpoint) return;


_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 46–47
const owner = cfg.owner || "the site owner";
const contact = safeLink(cfg.contact);


_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 49–54
const root =
  document.querySelector("main#quarto-document-content") ||
  document.querySelector("main") ||
  document.body;

let current = null; // { quote, section, anchor }

current holds the passage, section and anchor the next comment will be about.

The Comment buttons
There is one floating button, moved to each new selection. Pressing a mouse button normally clears the selection, so mousedown is cancelled to keep the highlighted text in place until the click opens the dialog.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 56–66
// ---- Floating "Comment" button shown next to a selection ----
const selBtn = el("button", {
  type: "button",
  class: "ic-select-btn",
  "aria-label": "Comment on the selected text",
  text: "💬 Comment",
});
selBtn.hidden = true;
selBtn.addEventListener("mousedown", (e) =&gt; e.preventDefault()); // keep the selection
selBtn.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; current && openDialog(current));
document.body.appendChild(selBtn);

With page-button: true there is also a fixed button for comments about the page as a whole, which have no passage and use the page title as the section.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 68–74
if (cfg.pageButton) {
  const pageBtn = el("button", { type: "button", class: "ic-page-btn", text: "💬 Comment on this page" });
  pageBtn.addEventListener("click", () =&gt;
    openDialog({ quote: "", section: document.title, anchor: "" })
  );
  document.body.appendChild(pageBtn);
}

Building the dialog
The dialog is a native &lt;dialog&gt; element, which the browser keeps keyboard focus inside and closes with Escape. It is built once and reused. The edit stage has the passage, the name and comment fields, the passcode field when enabled, and the honeypot: a field placed off-screen and hidden from screen readers, so only a bot fills it in.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 76–83
// ---- Dialog: edit stage ----
const quoteBox = el("blockquote", { class: "ic-quote" });
const sectionLine = el("p", { class: "ic-section" });
const nameInput = el("input", { type: "text", name: "name", required: true, maxlength: "80", autocomplete: "name" });
const commentInput = el("textarea", { name: "comment", required: true, maxlength: "5000", rows: "6" });
const passInput = el("input", { type: "password", name: "passcode", autocomplete: "off", required: !!cfg.passcode });
// Honeypot: hidden from people, often filled in by spam bots.
const trap = el("input", { type: "text", name: "website", tabindex: "-1", autocomplete: "off", class: "ic-trap", "aria-hidden": "true" });

The notice states who posts the issue, what becomes public, and that only the owner can change or remove it.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 85–96
const contactText = () =&gt;
  contact
    ? el("a", { href: contact, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", text: "contact " + owner })
    : document.createTextNode("contact " + owner);

const notice = el("p", { class: "ic-note" }, [
  "Comments are posted publicly as GitHub issues by " + owner +
    ", together with your name, the highlighted text and a link to this section. " +
    "You can't edit or delete a comment after posting; to change or remove one, ",
  contactText(),
  ".",
]);


_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 98–106
const editStage = el("div", {}, [
  sectionLine,
  quoteBox,
  el("label", {}, ["Your name", nameInput]),
  el("label", {}, ["Comment", commentInput]),
]);
if (cfg.passcode) editStage.appendChild(el("label", {}, ["Passcode", passInput]));
editStage.appendChild(trap);
editStage.appendChild(notice);

The preview stage is an empty card that fillPreview fills in. Both stages, the status line and the two action buttons share one form.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 108–126
// ---- Dialog: preview stage ----
const pvTitle = el("p", { class: "ic-pv-title" });
const pvBody = el("div", { class: "ic-pv-body" });
const previewStage = el("div", { class: "ic-preview" }, [
  el("p", { class: "ic-section", text: "This is exactly what will be posted publicly on GitHub by " + owner + ":" }),
  el("div", { class: "ic-pv-card" }, [pvTitle, pvBody]),
]);

const status = el("p", { class: "ic-status", role: "status" });
const backBtn = el("button", { type: "button", text: "Cancel" });
const goBtn = el("button", { type: "submit", class: "ic-primary" });

const heading = el("h2", { text: "Leave a comment" });
const form = el("form", { method: "dialog" }, [
  heading, editStage, previewStage, status,
  el("div", { class: "ic-actions" }, [backBtn, goBtn]),
]);
const dialog = el("dialog", { class: "ic-dialog" }, [form]);
document.body.appendChild(dialog);

Moving between stages
The dialog is always in one of three stages. setStage shows the right parts and relabels the two buttons for each, so the left button is Cancel, then Edit, then Close, and the right is Preview or Post publicly. The left button goes back from the preview, and otherwise closes the dialog.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 128–145
let stage = "edit"; // "edit" | "preview" | "done"

function setStage(s) {
  stage = s;
  editStage.hidden = s !== "edit";
  previewStage.hidden = s !== "preview";
  goBtn.hidden = s === "done";
  goBtn.disabled = false;
  heading.textContent = s === "preview" ? "Check before posting" : "Leave a comment";
  backBtn.textContent = s === "edit" ? "Cancel" : s === "preview" ? "Edit" : "Close";
  goBtn.textContent = s === "edit" && cfg.preview ? "Preview" : "Post publicly";
  if (s !== "done") { status.textContent = ""; status.className = "ic-status"; }
}

backBtn.addEventListener("click", () =&gt; {
  if (stage === "preview") setStage("edit");
  else dialog.close();
});

openDialog loads a passage into the dialog and resets it. Focus goes to the comment box when the name is already remembered, and to the name box otherwise.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 147–158
function openDialog(ctx) {
  current = ctx;
  selBtn.hidden = true;
  quoteBox.textContent = ctx.quote;
  quoteBox.hidden = !ctx.quote;
  sectionLine.textContent = ctx.section ? "Section: " + ctx.section : "";
  nameInput.value = store(NAME_KEY);
  commentInput.value = "";
  setStage("edit");
  dialog.showModal();
  (nameInput.value ? commentInput : nameInput).focus();
}

fillPreview draws the issue as it will appear. It mirrors the text buildIssue_ writes on the relay, without the section link.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 160–168
function fillPreview(name, comment) {
  pvTitle.textContent = issueTitle(current.section, comment);
  pvBody.replaceChildren(
    el("p", {}, [el("strong", { text: "Submitted by: " }), name + ", via the site's comment form"]),
    el("p", {}, [el("strong", { text: "Section: " }), current.section || "Page"]),
    ...(current.quote ? [el("blockquote", { class: "ic-quote", text: current.quote })] : []),
    el("p", { class: "ic-pv-comment", text: comment })
  );
}

Posting
post sends the comment and shows the result. The body is JSON, but labelled text/plain: a JSON content type would make the browser send a CORS preflight request first, which Apps Script web apps cannot answer, and the post would be blocked. Apps Script follows a redirect to return its reply, which fetch follows automatically. Any { ok: false } reply is shown to the reader as the relay’s own message.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 170–211
async function post(name, comment) {
  const payload = {
    name,
    comment,
    quote: current.quote,
    section: current.section,
    anchor: current.anchor,
    page: location.origin + location.pathname,
    passcode: passInput.value,
    website: trap.value,
  };
  goBtn.disabled = true;
  backBtn.disabled = true;
  status.textContent = "Posting…";
  status.className = "ic-status";
  try {
    // text/plain avoids a CORS preflight, which Apps Script cannot answer.
    const res = await fetch(cfg.endpoint, {
      method: "POST",
      headers: { "Content-Type": "text/plain;charset=utf-8" },
      body: JSON.stringify(payload),
    });
    const data = await res.json();
    if (!data.ok) throw new Error(data.error || "The comment was not posted.");
    setStage("done");
    status.className = "ic-status ic-ok";
    status.replaceChildren(
      "Thanks! Posted as ",
      data.url
        ? el("a", { href: data.url, target: "_blank", rel: "noopener", text: "issue #" + data.number })
        : "an issue",
      "."
    );
    window.getSelection().removeAllRanges();
  } catch (err) {
    status.textContent = err.message || "Something went wrong. Please try again.";
    status.className = "ic-status ic-error";
    goBtn.disabled = false;
  } finally {
    backBtn.disabled = false;
  }
}

The form’s submit handler runs the browser’s own validation of the required fields, tidies the name, remembers it, and then either shows the preview or posts.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 213–227
form.addEventListener("submit", (e) =&gt; {
  e.preventDefault();
  if (stage === "edit" && !form.reportValidity()) return;
  const name = nameInput.value.trim().replace(/\s+/g, " ");
  const comment = commentInput.value.trim();
  if (!name || !comment) return;
  store(NAME_KEY, name);
  if (stage === "edit" && cfg.preview) {
    fillPreview(name, comment);
    setStage("preview");
    goBtn.focus();
  } else {
    post(name, comment);
  }
});

Tracking the selection
sectionFor finds the section a passage belongs to. Quarto wraps each heading and its content in a &lt;section&gt; with the heading’s id, so the nearest enclosing section[id] gives both the heading text and the anchor for the issue’s link.

_extensions/issue-comments/issue-comments.js, lines 230–238
function sectionFor(node) {
  const start = node.nodeType === 1 ? node : node.parentElement;
  const section = start && start.closest("section[id]");
  if (section) {
    const h = section.querySelector(":scope &gt; h1, :scope &gt; h2, :scope &gt; h3, :scope &gt; h4, :scope &gt; h5, :scope &gt; h6");
    return { section: h ? h.textContent.trim() : section.id, anchor: section.id };
  }
  return { section: document.title, anchor: "" };
}

update decides whether to show the button and where. It ignores selections while the dialog is open, ones shorter than two characters, and ones outside the main content. The button goes just under the last line of the selection, kept inside the window.

